  "query_preview": "../through Sunday night/ \nPrimary focus continues to be on warm/dry through the end of the \nweek along with the uncertainty of equatorial tropical storms and \nsubsequent moisture transport into the N/Pac. Starting off, for \nthe end of the week clusters still show strong agreement in a \nridge dominating the western seaboard, limiting major surface lows\nfrom moving into the Gulf and serving as the source for \nabnormally warm temperatures aloft. LREF 850mb temperature \nhistograms have shifted to more normal distribution, translating \nto increased confidence for 12C to 14C at 5000ft by Thur/Fri. For \nfolks at sea level, this means warm temperatures, with afternoon \nhighs likely in the mid-70s to mid-80s, depending on where you are\nin the Panhandle. Thunderstorms might make an appearance Thur/Fri\nacross the central and southern area; however, this will \nultimately depend on where the ridge axis sets up and a shortwave \nmoving down from Canada along the coast range, something to watch.\n\nMoving through the weekend guidance breaks down the ridge \nsuggesting cooler temperatures and some moisture; however, \nguidance continues to break down the ridge too soon. Thinking we \nhold on to warmer temperatures into Saturday, with cooler \ntemperatures and moisture arriving by late Sunday and/or start of \nnext week. With that said, there are a variety of outcomes in \nvarious ensemble system solutions; current forecast Monday \nafternoon sticks with troughing setting up in the Gulf and an \nincrease in clouds and light rain. \n\nFor the maritime environment, anticipate northwest winds to \ncontinue along the coast, with daily sea breezes moving through \nthe inside, peak afternoon speeds likely moderate to fresh \nbreezes. By Sunday/Monday northwest winds along the coast give way\nto southerlies as a series of lows moves into the Gulf. Will need\nto watch for the potential for fresh to strong breezes along the \nnorthern coast.",
